name bfm-blind-1
timeout 300                                  # sim-seconds until TIMEOUT (by design — see bfm-basic.fbm)

# THE SENSOR-LIMITATION CASE. Same defender and same defined manoeuvre as bfm-basic.fbm (a sustained
# max-rate left turn around one unreachable waypoint), same datalink-off radar-only pursuer. What changes
# is the entry: viper starts 2 nm ahead of bandit and 300 m off his track, pointing back at him, so the
# two pass each other CLOSE ABEAM at some 800 kt of combined speed.
#
# That pass BREAKS THE RADAR LOCK, and no amount of piloting can prevent it: at a few hundred metres the
# line of sight swings through 180 deg in about a second, so the target leaves the antenna's +/-60 deg STT
# gimbal envelope whatever the nose is doing. It is not a defect and not a mission switch turning the
# radar off — it is what an active sensor on a gimbal does, and it is the reason "keep the radar on him"
# is a manoeuvre goal in its own right in systems/FBPilot's BFM phase.
#
# What this mission is here to show, in events.log + the bfm_* telemetry columns:
#   1. RADAR_LOST at the pass;
#   2. the pilot does NOT go blind — systems/FBBfmTrack keeps propagating the last known vector, bfm_valid
#      stays 1 while bfm_age counts up, and the nose keeps following the prediction;
#   3. once the prediction is older than the pilot's BfmScanAfterS the scan weaves, and once it is older
#      than FBBfmTrack::kMaxExtrapolateS the pilot stops believing it and flies back towards the BEARING
#      and ALTITUDE it last saw him at, scanning (bfm_pursuit = search);
#   4. RADAR_LOCK again, and the fight resumes into the control position.
# The blind interval between (1) and (4) is the honest cost of having no eyes, and it is long — but its
# LENGTH is not a stable number and must not be read as one. Measured across a six-value sweep of the
# BFM roll-rate cap (60…105 deg/s, doc/pilot.md 5.7) the same file reacquires at t=50.9, t=120.0 or
# t=209.0 with nothing monotone in between: the pass throws the search into one of a few basins, and any
# perturbation of the roll law reshuffles which. On the committed law it is RADAR_LOST at t=9.9 and
# RADAR_LOCK again at t=209.0. What is reproducible is the SEQUENCE (1)-(4), and that is the claim.
#
# THE ROLL EXTENT BOUND (doc/pilot.md 5.7.3) moves this file's trace and NOT its two named times: still
# RADAR_LOST t=9.9, still RADAR_LOCK t=209.0, and the sequence (1)-(4) complete. What moves is what
# happens AFTER the reacquisition — a second loss at t=239.0 and a third lock at t=276.0 — and with it
# the run's integrals: bfm_lock_s 99.1 -> 50.4 and bfm_ctrl_s 48.4 -> 0.0. That is CAUSAL and not chaos
# (perturbing the spawn by 0.8 m steps over +-3 m gives 0.0 in all seven samples on the new law and 48.4
# in six of seven on the old), and it is the DECLARED COST of the bound: a sustained roll is now half the
# peak, so the long one-direction conversion this file's second engagement needs is flown slower and does
# not arrive. The mission's own claim is untouched — it is about going blind and coming back, not about
# the kill it never had. The flying itself got strictly quieter: peak roll 103.4 -> 98.7 deg/s, roll per
# 2 s window 112.3 -> 111.8 deg, longest stretch above the monitor's 60 deg/s threshold 1.2 -> 0.8 s.

unit viper
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.67755 6.94854 4000 270.0 380      # 2 nm ahead of bandit, 300 m off his track, pointing at him
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode acm_hud
  set task bfm

unit bandit
  module f16
  team friendly
  spawn 46.67485 6.90000 4000 90.0 300
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set datalink off
  set fcr_mode off
  wp 46.70000 6.90000 4000 300               # the CENTRE of its turn — see bfm-basic.fbm's header
